# Paste everything below this line as one prompt

---

Convert Creo from a single-agency application into a multi-tenant platform. The platform
owner (super admin) sells Creo to marketing agencies; each agency manages its own teams,
staff and clients and can never see another agency's data.

This is the largest change in the project. Read all of it before writing code, and
complete Stage 0 before touching a file.

---

# 1. Corrections to the org model as drafted

Three errors in the source architecture. Fix them before building.

### 1.1 "One team handles one client" — drop it

The source diagrams contradict each other: one shows Team 1 → Clients A, B, C, D; the
other shows Team 1 → one client. The rule also collapses the moment members serve other
clients, which the same document requires.

**The fix is to separate two relations that were conflated:**

- **Ownership** — a client belongs to exactly one team. Accountability, reporting,
  escalation. One team can own many clients.
- **Assignment** — a client has one named person per required role. Those people
  usually come from the owning team, but may come from any team in the agency when the
  owning team lacks capacity.

Once separated, "Team 1 owns Clients A–D" and "each client has a named editor" are both
true and no longer conflict.

### 1.2 Capacity is measured in points, never in clients

"Video Editor capacity = 3 clients" cannot work, because clients are not equal:

```
Monthly effort points per client, by role

role                 Starter   Accelerator   Enterprise
video_editor              20            40           80
designer                  26            50          100
videographer               8             8           16
content_creator           11            22           43
social_manager            11            22           43

An editor with a 100-point monthly budget carries:
  5.00 Starter clients  |  2.50 Accelerator  |  1.25 Enterprise
```

Same person, same "capacity", 4× difference. Store `monthly_points` per member and
consume points per client per plan.

```python
EFFORT = {"reel": 5, "poster": 2, "story": 1, "shoot_day": 8, "item_overhead": 0.5}

def client_load(plan) -> dict[str, int]:
    items = plan.posters + plan.reels + plan.stories
    return {
      "video_editor":    plan.reels   * EFFORT["reel"],
      "graphic_designer":plan.posters * EFFORT["poster"] + plan.stories * EFFORT["story"],
      "videographer":    plan.shoot_days * EFFORT["shoot_day"],
      "content_creator": round(items * EFFORT["item_overhead"]),
      "social_manager":  round(items * EFFORT["item_overhead"]),
    }
```

> Note for staffing: under these weights the **graphic designer is the bottleneck in
> every tier**, not the video editor. Posters plus stories outnumber reels heavily.
> Surface this in the admin capacity view.

### 1.3 Bottleneck is computed over required roles only

```python
def team_capacity(team, plan, required_roles: set[str]) -> tuple[float, str]:
    load = client_load(plan)
    ratios = {
        role: team.available_points(role) / load[role]
        for role in required_roles if load[role] > 0
    }
    bottleneck = min(ratios, key=ratios.get)
    return ratios[bottleneck], bottleneck
```

A client needing only editing and design must not be blocked by videographer capacity.

---

# 2. The tenancy decision

**Shared database, shared schema, `agency_id` on every tenant-owned table, PostgreSQL
Row-Level Security as the safety net.** This is the right default for a B2B SaaS at
100–10,000 tenants; Notion runs the same pattern keyed on workspace_id. Schema-per-tenant
and database-per-tenant are migration and cost burdens you do not need yet.

**Application filtering alone is not a security boundary.** One forgotten predicate, one
over-broad repository method, one background job, one ad-hoc query crosses the boundary —
and isolation bugs do not announce themselves; they leak quietly for weeks until a
pen test or a curious user finds them. So build both layers:

| Layer | Mechanism | Catches |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | Repository requires `scope: TenantScope` — cannot be called without it | Developer forgetting a filter |
| 2 | Postgres RLS policy on every tenant table | Everything layer 1 misses |

### 2.1 The PgBouncer trap — read this twice

You run Supabase's pooler in **transaction mode**. Session variables are reset between
transactions, so `SET app.agency_id` at connection time silently stops working under
load, and RLS then sees an empty tenant and returns nothing — or, if your policy is
written permissively, everything.

**Set the tenant context with `SET LOCAL`, inside the same transaction as the query:**

```python
@asynccontextmanager
async def tenant_session(agency_id: UUID, role: str):
    async with async_session() as db:
        async with db.begin():                       # one transaction
            await db.execute(
                text("SELECT set_config('app.agency_id', :aid, true)"),  # true = LOCAL
                {"aid": str(agency_id)},
            )
            await db.execute(
                text("SELECT set_config('app.user_role', :r, true)"),
                {"r": role},
            )
            yield db
```

Every request and every Celery task opens its session this way. A task that does not
carry tenant context must not be able to run — see §6.

### 2.2 Connect as a non-superuser

RLS is bypassed by table owners and superusers. Supabase's service key connects as a
role that bypasses it. Create a dedicated application role:

```sql
CREATE ROLE creo_app NOLOGIN;
GRANT USAGE ON SCHEMA public TO creo_app;
GRANT SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE ON ALL TABLES IN SCHEMA public TO creo_app;
ALTER ROLE creo_app SET row_security = on;
-- the app connects as creo_app. NOT as postgres, NOT via the service key.
```

If the app still connects as a bypassing role, **RLS is decoration**. Verify it: connect
as the app role, unset `app.agency_id`, and confirm `SELECT * FROM clients` returns zero
rows.

